in this video we'll talk about  electroencephalogram  or eeg  this is a non-invasive method to record  the macroscopic electrical activity by  placing electrodes on the scalp  eeg recordings represent the activity of  the surface layer in the brain  now  electroencephalogram is basically the  output of the eeg machine and these  graphs represent the electrical activity  coming from the brain  during this diagnostic test a small  sensor is placed all across the scalp  to record the electrical activity coming  underneath the skull and this is  generally non-invasive and painless  these signals are recorded by a machine  and the doctor can interpret the meaning  of these signal in this video we'll try  to understand what does this signal mean  how this test is performed and what is  the biological and physical basis behind  this test  so let's talk about where are these  electrode placed actually these  electrodes are placed using a  conventional system of 10 20 rule  and in a moment it would be clear what  is this convention  but let me tell you these are broadly  placed on the scalp  and the neurons which are active just  underneath the scalp that means in the  cortical layer their activity can be  recorded using  eeg  now the  waveform that is coming out of a  encephalogram actually represent or  reflects the electrical activity of  cortical neurons  the signal intensity is very small  and  these uh  particular fluctuations are in order of  microvolts that means it's very small  now it detects population level of  neuronal activity  it does not depict one single neurons  activity now let's try to understand the  placement of electrodes in bit more  details so this is the skull and we are  looking at the skull from a top view  here let's try to understand some  landmarks and this is how  a particular technician will put the  electrodes on the scalp  so  the particular end which is close to the  nose is known as nasion  so first  the nasion electrode is placed it's kind  of like a landmark  then the ionian electrode is placed  which is at the back of the scalp  and then  the pre  auricular points are detected which are  close to our ears and those electrodes  are placed and with respect to these  landmarks other electrodes are placed in  a specific  fashion  for example from the nasian electrode  the first  line that can connect the ionian and  nasian is known as the midline  the next electrode to be placed is  something arbitrary 10 units apart from  the nasian electrode  but once this particular electrode is  placed the next electrode is to be  placed 20 units or 20 percent distance  with respect to the uh previous one and  this is known as the 1020 rule  there are several rules of nomenclature  which would be important to understand  the data for example  you must be seeing these kind of  notations in the eeg recordings like fz  cz or pz  so p stands for parietal c stands for  central and if stands for frontal so all  these notation tells us where where  these electrodes are placed in the brain  and where does the activity is recorded  so it's kind of like a geography on the  brain  now t stands for temporal  here when we see t8 or c4 we have to  understand these recordings are taken  from the right half of the scalp whereas  if we see odd numbers like c3 or t9 we  have to understand these are recordings  coming from the left half of the brain  so these conventions are really  important to understand the traces now  if we look at a sample trace we can  clearly understand what does these  stress mean for example if p1 clearly  indicates that it is from  you guessed it correct from the frontal  part and it's from the left hemisphere  fp2 is from the frontal part but it is  from the right hemisphere  similarly c3 and c4 you can completely  understand t3 and t4 are coming from  temporal rope  so this is how the nomenclature is set  up  now let's try to understand exactly how  eeg works  so this is a portion of the scalp we can  look at the electrode here  and underneath the electrode there is  scalp and underneath the scalp there  would be meninges and beneath that there  will be the cortical layer where we can  see the neurons are there here the red  neurons are synapsed with these blue  neurons or the axonal terminals of these  blue neurons if we zoom into this axonal  terminal we can see that there are  neurotransmission going on  so in this particular excitatory synapse  whenever there is  electrical activity there would be  cation influx inside of these red  neurons or the dendrites of these red  neurons  so there would be net buildup of  positive charge inside the neuron but at  the same time there would be depletion  of the positive charge in the outside of  the neuron or the extracellular site of  the neuron and there would be a  development of negative charge on the  outer side and these kind of delta  changing charge is detected by the  electroencephalogram  so now we can understand when the charge  is now more negative  there would be a downward deflection in  the voltage that is detected by a eeg  trace  and this is recorded over time so what  we see as a trace is a recording of  voltage fluctuation over time and it can  be order of several minutes to several  hours  now let us try to understand how exactly  the eeg data is represented  the easy data is actually understood in  context of  a differential gating parameter  so here there are two inputs  in these two inputs are feed into this  eeg machine  and that would give or give rise to an  output but since it's a differential  amplifier kind of  machinery  we can see only the differences are  taken as an output if we see this input  1 and input 2 we can see the point of  difference here as a peak and this  differences is  the thing that would be  taken as an output  so now if we look at a particular  electroencephalograms  trace we can see  these kind of traces are known as  bipolar montage that means the  differences between two electro  electrodes are pointed out for example  fp1 and f3 means the differences between  these electrical activities in these two  sites  already if we look at these particular  recordings we can see  there are differences like the c3 and p3  the electrical activities are not so  much different that's why the  fluctuations are less  now in terms of fp1 and fp f3 we can see  the differences are quite uh stark and  that's why there are a lot of  fluctuations in the traces there are  many other ways of representing the  electroencephalogram data but in this  video we are not going to focus on that  and complicate things  okay let's talk about the  important aspect of eeg  eeg recordings  depicts the population level activity  that means it's kind of tell us  overall activity of a brain area it does  not point out how a single neuron  behaves  if we want to understand how a single  neuron behave we have to do  intracellular recording using specific  electrodes  and if you want to learn more about  intracellular recordings you can click  on the video in the i button  but overall easy recordings give us an  idea about the population level  macroscopic activity now there are  several signatures in the eeg waveforms  such as delta theta alpha beta and all  of these thing has different frequencies  for example delta has a frequency range  of one to four hertz whereas gamma has a  frequency of more than 35 hertz and all  of these signature has their own meaning  some signatures are predominant when the  individual is awake and some signatures  are predominant when the individual is  asleep  so these kind of signatures means a lot  in terms of brain activity  a trend doctor can understand what does  these signatures actually mean in terms  of a clinical point of view  now electroencephalogram could be a very  good diagnostic tool to detect epilepsy  especially if we look at a normal  individual the eeg traces would look  like this but once an individual is  undergoing a seizure or epileptic  seizure  the electroencephalogram would  completely change and a huge amount of  activity would be visualized using this  kind of method  now electroencephalogram can also be  used to diagnose sleep related disorder  in different phases of sleep there are  different electroencephalogram signals  so one can try to match these  electroencephalogram signals to  determine what changes happened in sleep  quality and  especially it is important for people  who are insomniac and suffering from  other kind of sleep  disabilities  it can also be useful for diagnosis of  depression  anxiety related behaviors and many other  things  but there are certain disadvantages  associated with these method so let's  review that  so the advantages are plenty for example  it's good for monitoring population  level neural activity in behaving humans  also it is non-invasive in nature so  it's pretty easy to do  but there are certain disadvantages like  poor resolution  it cannot tell the activity at a single  neuron resolution it can only talk about  a population level of activity it can  only tell us that whether our brain area  is active inactive and how much that  activity is  so it's a ballpark estimate not absolute  one  it just gives us an overview of activity  but no intricate details are provided  from the electroencephalogram recordings  so i hope this video was useful if you  like this video give it a quick thumbs  up  get notes and more flashcards in my  facebook page link is in the description  don't forget to follow that  also you can support my channel by  clicking on this super like option your  small contribution means a lot for me  anyway if you need more dynamic flash  cards you can definitely visit my  facebook page you can download  animations that would help in your  presentation or understanding  my courses are also present in an  academy which is india's biggest online  learning platform using a code ap ap10  you can get a 10 discount see you in  next video

